```rust
fn find_sysml_in_dir(dir: &Path) -> Option<PathBuf> {
    if let Ok(entries) = fs::read_dir(dir) {
        let mut sysml_files = Vec::new();
        for entry in entries.flatten() {
            let p = entry.path();
            if p.is_file() && p.extension().map_or(false, |ext| ext == "sysml") {
                sysml_files.push(p);
            }
        }
        sysml_files.sort();
        if let Some(first) = sysml_files.into_iter().next() {
            return Some(first);
        }
    }
    None
}
```

This implementation introduces three correctness and traceability defects:

1. Non-Recursive Directory Scanning: `fs::read_dir` reads only direct descendants of `dir`. Subdirectories (such as `schema/subsystems/`, `schema/domain/`, or `schema/conops/`) fail `p.is_file()` on line 347 and are skipped entirely. The function never traverses subtrees.
2. Arbitrary Single-File Truncation: On lines 351-354, if multiple `.sysml` files exist in `dir` (e.g. `01_conops.sysml`, `02_avionics.sysml`), `sysml_files.sort()` orders them lexicographically and extracts only the first file via `sysml_files.into_iter().next()`. All subsequent schema files are silently discarded.
3. Silent False-Positive Gate Clearance: In `run_compilation_gate` (`crates/compile-sysml/src/main.rs:225-316`), the compiler reads only the single file returned by `resolve_schema_file`. It validates and serializes that single package to `.pipeline/schema.sysml`, emitting `Successfully compiled SysML schema '...'` and returning exit code 0.

Invariants Violated:
